**Convention 11.1 (precise reuse and literature boundaries).** The connected cover, strict full-constraint response, event classification and original-class deductions in §§2–10 are `repo-derived` conditional mathematics. Source/root/rank/TP and minimax are reused only from CG §§13–17; CG17's averaged-live scope is not enlarged. Sharp cost and the unaveraged equality face come from JR34–42, and actual-label capacity, endpoint splitting and simultaneous construction from FA69–72 and corrected RC67/75. The selected-root and local transition results of [SC][SC] do not alone supply the interval proof. The interval data below, the full-circle inequalities and all joins supply the additional continuum connection.

The following published tools have their stated mathematical scope. Dette–Melas, *A note on the de la Garza phenomenon for locally optimal designs*, arXiv:1105.3575v1, §3 Theorem 1 (journal Theorem 3.1), requires a specified ordinary Chebyshev system and preserves designated information entries with a Loewner improvement. Proposition 7.3 violates that ordinary premise; Loewner improvement does not establish this full physical covariance/objective preservation. Martin–Goldsztejn–Granvilliers–Jermann, *On Continuation Methods for Non-Linear Bi-Objective Optimization: Towards a Certified Interval-Based Approach*, DOI 10.1007/s10898-014-0201-3, §§3.1–3.3, treats regular certified continuation under its $C^2$ and active-set crossing hypotheses; it does not supply this global dual or all components. Hauenstein–Mohammad-Nezhad–Tang–Terlaky, *On computing the nonlinearity interval in parametric semidefinite optimization*, arXiv:1908.10499v2, Theorems 3.10 and 4.3, Proposition 4.8 and Example 3.11, uses independent constraint matrices, strict primal/dual feasibility and the stated nonsingularity hypotheses. In particular interval-wide optimal regularity in Theorem 4.3 and a nonsingular entire complex KKT fibre at the parameter in Proposition 4.8 cannot be replaced by one nonsingular displayed solution; a singular non-transition point is possible. No actual-label/SDP-rank identification is borrowed. Halaseh–Magron–Skomra, *Duality attainment and strict feasibility of the generalized moment problem and its relaxations*, arXiv:2604.15072v1, Theorem 1.1, assumes $h_1=1$, $b_1>0$ and relative-interior moment data, with additional algebraic compactness for its SOS consequences; attainment does not evaluate an ordered atlas. These are literature-attested intermediate frameworks with no imported global theorem used in place of the explicit proof here. Their retrieval provenance and worldwide novelty are not mathematical premises of this volume.

